## Job

An act names a calling.
The calling has four parts: who answers, who does, who checks, and what is given first.
An act with no calling is not run. Compute kicks it back.
The three hands are compute, model, and user.
The user is the director on this desk. Authority stays with a person who can assume the risk.

## Compute

Compute is the mechanical hand.
It works from consistent rules and from the books the desk is holding.
It does not guess.
When the rules and the books decide the act, compute does it.
A miss goes to the model with a top list, so the model can pick without interrupting the user.
A miss by the model goes to the user. Authority stops with the user.
A later fork is for compute only when the specification is exact.
It stamps the header, the version, the address, and the hash.
It takes the next free slot. It builds a menu from headings that are already written.
Derived faces are compute's work. They can be written without a user check.
A derived face records its author: compute, model, or user. A model author says so.
If a derived face is missing or not working, the miss goes up for a check.
The director answers the rules for derived faces.
It stops when two faces disagree.
It may write `status: closed` while `reply:` is still blank.
After a run, the model supplies the status. Compute writes what was done on each `#NN` before it closes the task.

## Model

The model fills the why and the body.
It may propose. It may write the next face 06 note.
The header `who` is the writer. `caller:` is a line on the ask.
The pages live in RAM with compute. The model requests what it needs, in a compact form, from compute.
Before the model starts, compute hands it a cold boot for the book it is using.
Each book is a local environment. Pages `0001` through `0009` are reserved so a book can port a rule from the kernel and apply a local change.
The model writes the work it did on `status:`.
`reply:` is the user only.
A prime may answer a worker inside its grant on `status:`. That line is never a reply.

## User

The user is the person who can assume the risk.
Right now that person is the director.
The hand on a desk is whoever holds that desk.
The kernel and the workshop stay with the director.
Sign is a compute and model step. It checks that the page is finished and functional.
In dev mode, that check keeps the work from moving on without the director.
Approve, lock, and promote stay with the user.
A reply is for the user. A release is for the user.
Handing an act down to a model is local work. The user engages that mode. Compute activates it. The shape of that mode is still open.

## Place

`caller:` sits on the ask.
The calling is read from the command list: who answers, who does, who checks.
Every write is stamped with its hand.
Where this rule is seated is still open.

## Open

An author line on each section is still open.
How a power user differs from an average user is still open.
The sign line in `AGENTS` is still open.
What `>keep` means is still open.

## Pair

Law `0040` says the director answers by number. This page uses that.
Law `0056` was not edited.
Page `0100.model` is the model door after a full band. It is a different page.

